  "reason": "Evidence: The current best is config_019 (multimodal, multiprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, avgpool, weighted) at 0.639 acc. The image modality in isolation is very weak (config_010: 0.208 acc with resnet50), yet multimodal still outperforms RNA-only (0.591), suggesting the image adds some signal. However, we have only tested one image encoder (resnet50), which is a relatively basic CNN. The imagenet_vit_l encoder is a substantially more powerful pretrained vision model and is completely untested. Question: Does upgrading the image encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l improve multimodal performance, or is the image modality's contribution fundamentally limited regardless of encoder quality? Why this is the best next experiment: config_025 is a clean single-variable ablation from the current best (config_019 → config_025, changing only image_encoder from resnet50 to imagenet_vit_l). This directly tests whether the image encoder is the bottleneck in the multimodal pipeline. The result will either unlock a new performance ceiling (if ViT-L helps) or confirm that the image modality's contribution is capped (if it doesn't), both of which are critical for deciding whether to invest further in image-related design choices (aggregation, fusion) or pivot toward RNA encoder improvements (scvi, scgpt)."
